Let $\Omega$ be a Polish space and let $C_b(\Omega)$ denote the Banach space of bounded continuous real-valued functions on $\Omega$ equipped with the supremum norm. Call a one-parameter family $T=(T(t))_{t\ge 0}$ of linear contractions on $C_b(\Omega)$ a transition semigroup if:

(i) $T(0)=I$ and $T(t+s)=T(t)T(s)$ for all $t,s\ge 0$;
(ii) for every $f\in C_b(\Omega)$ and every $s\ge 0$, $T(t)f\to T(s)f$ locally uniformly on $\Omega$ as $t\to s$;
(iii) for every $t\ge 0$ and every uniformly bounded sequence $(f_n)_{n\in\mathbb N}\subset C_b(\Omega)$ that converges locally uniformly to some $f\in C_b(\Omega)$, the sequence $T(t)f_n$ converges locally uniformly to $T(t)f$.

The generator of such a semigroup is defined (with a suitable choice of domain consistent with the locally-uniform convergence above) as the operator $Af=\lim_{t\downarrow 0}(T(t)f-f)/t$.

Problem. Provide a characterization, analogous to the classical Lumer–Phillips theorem (which states that a densely defined dissipative operator $A$ on a Banach space generates a strongly continuous contraction semigroup if and only if $\mathrm{range}(\lambda_0 I - A)$ is the whole space for some $\lambda_0>0$), of those linear operators $A$ on $C_b(\Omega)$ that arise as generators of transition semigroups in the sense above.

